WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.320
In the office of Paul’s editor, we see cover
art for several Misery books.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.660
This is the book series that made him famous,
and caused his self proclaimed number one

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.040
fan to keep him prisoner after he breaks his
legs.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.730
This one is a French version of Misery’s
Love, one of the four Misery books specifically

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.570
mentioned in the novel by Stephen King.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.509
Misery’s Quest is another, and we can see
that cover right here.

00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:20.700
The first Misery book is called The Adventures
of Misery Chastain in the movie,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.620
it’s a little
easier to see later on in the film.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.280
We also get a look at the cover of the
original finale of the Misery series, Misery’s

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:26.940
Child.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.260
Annie would force Paul to write a sequel to
Misery’s Child, which would be known as

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:33.530
Misery’s Return, and in the pages we see
him writing, there are some easter eggs you

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.740
might have missed, stick around to the end
of this video to hear about them.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.580
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:46.899
Today I’m covering the 1990 film adaptation
of Stephen King’s novel Misery.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:50.579
If you haven’t figured that out already,
that’s what we’re doing today.

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:53.970
King was not really all that down to sell
the movie rights to Misery at first, which

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.670
I find kind of ironic because the main character
kind of considers himself a sell-out, but

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.230
according to Mental Floss he ended up changing
his mind because he liked what director Rob

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.860
Reiner did in adapting his novella, The Body,
into the movie known as Stand by Me; which

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.460
is a non-horror movie so I probably won’t
cover it on my channel, but there is one scene

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.540
that’s truly horrifying.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:14.380
"Leaches!"

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.980
*screaming*

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:17.580
"Get 'em off!"

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.220
"Get 'em off me!"

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.760
Anyway… let’s get back to Misery.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.870
The movie opens with Paul finishing up his
next novel.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.340
He’s finally free of making Misery books
after killing the character in the previous

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.880
one, so he’s moved onto something more serious.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.520
We can see some of the text on the final page
of this book.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.520
It reads:

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.090
“‘I better not take that for granted anymore,’
he told himself.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.630
Breathing might not seem like much, but without
it, what else was there…?”

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.430
This is Paul not practicing what he preaches,
not yet at least, because at the end of the

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.360
movie, he’ll eventually admit that he was
taking his life for granted before his experience

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.150
with Annie Wilkes.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:15.080
“In some way, Annie Wilkes… that whole
experience… helped me.”

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.750
Annie tries to make him burn this book later
on, and in doing so, we get a glimpse of the

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.750
first page.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.170
But it actually looks more like an essay about
teen movies, and mentioned Say Anything, Risky

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.780
Business, The Graduate and Fast Times At Ridgemont
High.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.370
After finishing this new book, Paul leaves
the hotel he was writing it at and his car

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.930
has a VKY 722 license plate, which is a common
plate seen in a lot of movies, like The Freshman,

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:39.310
Shaft and The Wolf of Wall Street, there are
probably others, I guess this is like the

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.370
Wilhelm Scream of license plates.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.790
Just before heading off, he throws a snowball
at a tree, which may be a small nod to the

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.230
fact that he played High School baseball in
the novel, where he supposedly couldn’t

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:49.040
hit a curve.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.920
And I wouldn’t be Emo Abraham Lincoln if
i didn’t mention this part.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.480
“I was a writer then.”

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.480
“You’re still a writer.”

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.740
“I haven’t been a writer since I got in
the Misery business.”

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.830
“I’m in the business of Misery, let’s
take it from the top.”

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:07.330
Earlier I mentioned that we get to see the
cover art of Misery’s Child in the film,

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:11.060
and when we see it later, you’ll also notice
the publisher is Viking Press, who in real

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.490
life at the time, published several Stephen
King novels such as The Dead Zone, Firestarter,

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.840
Cujo, Christine, The Talisman, The Eyes of
the Dragon, IT and course: Misery.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.420
When Paul wakes up in Annie’s house after
the crash, she tells him where he is.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.450
“We’re just outside Silver Creek.”

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.820
This is changed from the fictional town of
Sidewinder, Colorado in the book.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:32.760
Silver Creek is a real town, in fact, it was
a former mining town, which could be a clue

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.280
about the old miner’s practice of hobbling
their workers; a tactic that Annie uses on

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.280
her prisoner.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.600
There are also references to mining in some
of the newspapers seen on screen.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.310
Usually in Things You Missed, whenever there
are newspapers, there are easter eggs -- mainly

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.741
consisting of the art department of the movie
trying to sneak their names in there as the

00:03:48.741 --> 00:03:50.330
author of an article or something.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.670
There’s not a whole lot of that in Misery
though, and I think it’s because many of

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.931
the articles are just copied from real life
local newspapers in Nye County, Nevada where

00:03:57.931 --> 00:03:58.931
the movie was filmed.

00:03:58.931 --> 00:04:02.680
It actually shows up a couple times if you
pay close attention, even though the setting

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.870
of the movie is part of Clear Creek County,
in Colorado.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.340
The only crew reference easter egg I found
in the many newspapers that appear in Misery

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.319
is in the article about Annie being arrested
after the death of several babies in her maternity

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:17.539
ward, where it states her lawyer is named
Steven Popelka, who is likely a relative of

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:20.120
Joseph Popelka, a production assistant.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.110
Outside of the newspapers, there’s also
a sticky note in the Police Chief’s office

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:27.880
that says “call Carlsen”, a reference
to Charles Carlsen, the prop maker slash set dresser.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:28.560
And that is it!

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.620
So let’s go back to Annie’s house, because
it’s breakfast time.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.800
“I made you my specialty!

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.020
Scrambled eggs a la Wilkes.”

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.059
How is scrambled eggs your specialty?

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:42.610
I’m instantly reminded of this one line
in the book, where Paul is critical of her

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:43.240
cooking.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.860
“...everything she cooked came out tasting
strangely industrial, as if years of eating

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.569
in hospital cafeterias had somehow corrupted
any culinary talent she might once have had.”

00:04:51.569 --> 00:04:55.971
Not long after, she has her first explosive
episode, after reading about Misery’s death

00:04:55.971 --> 00:04:57.629
at the end of the final book.

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:00.860
She leaves to go away to her laughing place,
but before going, tells Paul,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.040
“You better hope nothing happens to me,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:05.920
because if I die,

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.580
you die.”

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.600
I feel like I’ve heard that somewhere before.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:17.340
“I think you missed the part that if he
dies… you die.”

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:18.490
Not quite, but pretty close.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:21.800
Paul sneaks out of the room and nearly breaks
a ceramic penguin, which is actually more

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.460
of a recurring symbol in the book, where Annie
uses it as an example when she says she knows

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.310
that Paul's been out of his room, the cops look
at it while inspecting the house in the summer,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.580
and near the very end, he actually throws
it through the parlor window to get their

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:33.580
attention.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.629
He also discovers a shrine dedicated to…
none other than himself, where Annie has a

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:41.820
signed photo and each of the nine Misery books,
with two copies of the first eight.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.339
As someone who has certain movies on both
DVD and Blu-Ray and probably will get 4Ks

00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:48.169
in the future, I can kind of relate.

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:51.569
He also snoops around in the kitchen, where
if you’re paying attention you may see another

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:55.349
connection to the book, in the form of this
calendar that is still stuck on February,

00:05:55.349 --> 00:05:57.409
because Annie keeps forgetting to turn the
pages.

00:05:57.409 --> 00:06:00.990
In the novel it’s in the guestroom Paul
is staying at, where it serves as a by-product

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:04.569
of her deteriorating mental state, but I guess
it’s in the kitchen for the film.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:07.349
One of the reasons that Paul snuck out of
the room was to get more medication, and he

00:06:07.349 --> 00:06:09.220
has a plan for what he wants to do with it.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.720
He empties the capsules into a small pocket
he folds using a pieces of notebook paper,

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:25.740
and tries to poison Annie with it at dinner.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.639
In the scene where he’s emptying out the
capsules, we see this strange camera angle.

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:31.159
I think this is Annie’s point of view.

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:34.589
There’s a part in the book where she looks
in through the keyhole, and that’s what

00:06:34.589 --> 00:06:38.380
I think is going on here and maybe the reason
she knocks over the glass of wine in the dinner

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.740
scene is actually no accident.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.099
After refilling the glass, they toast to the
return of the Misery character, and in the

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:46.860
days that follow, Paul gets down to work on
writing the novel.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.159
If you look at the pages in the typewriter,
many of them are actually accurate to the

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:51.300
text in the book.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.199
To be perfectly clear, Misery by Stephen King
has certain chapters that are just excerpts

00:06:55.199 --> 00:06:59.259
from Misery’s Return by Paul Sheldon, and
the text in the typewriter is often word for

00:06:59.260 --> 00:06:59.760
word.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.240
There are a couple of deviations, like this
part, which corresponds to page 169, but with

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.539
an added paragraph to account for the name
change in the movie, where Geoffrey is now

00:07:07.539 --> 00:07:08.539
known as Windthorne.

00:07:08.539 --> 00:07:10.629
I do not know the reason for this change though.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:14.839
Then there’s this shot, where the top paragraph
is pulled from page 168, but the bottom paragraph

00:07:14.839 --> 00:07:19.180
is new text, which says “As the flames crept
ever higher, Misery realized that only she

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.809
stood betwee…” something…

00:07:20.809 --> 00:07:24.909
I wonder if this was added as a foreshadowing
of the ending of the movie, where Misery is

00:07:24.909 --> 00:07:28.789
actually in flames, literally because Paul
burns the book, whereas in the novel he hides

00:07:28.789 --> 00:07:32.770
the real Misery’s Return and burns a blank
stack of pages to hit Annie where it hurts

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:33.420
most.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.760
*SCREAMING*

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.729
But as Paul progresses with the book, Annie
realizes that her time with Paul can’t last

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:45.210
forever, and falls into a depressive episode.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.219
That night, we see an exterior of the house
during a thunderstorm.

00:07:48.219 --> 00:07:49.520
The camera is crooked.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.759
This is called a dutch angle, and in this
case, it’s used to signal Annie is off balance

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:54.260
mentally.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:02.380
“You’re a beautiful, brilliant, famous
man of the world, and I’m not a movie star type."

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:05.380
Says Kathy Bates, the woman who would win
an Oscar for this role.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.340
"Kathy Bates in Misery."

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.980
"I would like to thank Jimmy Caan.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.960
and apologize publicly for the ankles."

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:25.600
Annie decides to go away again, and Paul sneaks
out of the room again, where he finds Annie’s

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.509
scrap book, and while these newspaper articles
documenting her life aren’t as reference-heavy

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:32.320
as I’d hoped, there is still info we can
pull from them.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.630
Like for example, if the movie feels like
it covers a shorter timespan than the book

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.169
it’s because it does.

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:41.250
The article claims that Paul’s vehicle was
found after 1 week, and this is not long before

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.000
the end of the movie at this point, so the
whole time he was at Annie’s in the movie

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.180
really only seems to cover a few weeks, maybe
a month, whereas in the novel he’s in captivity

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.310
for 5 months.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:54.630
This is one of the many examples of the movie
just being so much more tame and toned down.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.690
One movie change that I did like, which really
makes Annie that much scarier as an antagonist

00:08:58.690 --> 00:08:59.839
is this detail.

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:03.709
It says she’s was a valedictorian in high
school and graduated nursing school as a Magna

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:06.730
Cum Laude, meaning her grade point average
was almost perfect.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.991
So in addition to being this big, forceful
person she’s also very clever, and with

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.940
two broken legs, Paul’s only real hope is
to outsmart her in order to escape his situation.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:20.080
As you know if you watch my channel, I love
a good psychological battle in a horror movie.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.920
Another movie difference is that we actually
see the police chief trying to find him, rather

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.260
than just seeing everything from Paul’s
perspective.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.880
And he even has a little desk cowboy dressed
exactly like him.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.280
I wish I had a little desk cowboy version of me...

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:34.940
At some point the Colorado State Police get
involved in searching for Paul, and

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.519
Buster ends up on the helicopter to see if
he can spot anything out of the ordinary.

00:09:38.519 --> 00:09:43.360
Now Misery is a very Hitchcockian horror film,
in particular, it’s very similar to Alfred

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.250
Hitchcock’s 1954 film Rear Window, where
the main character is also confined to a single

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.740
room after breaking his leg.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:52.860
Hitchcock was known for almost always making
a cameo appearance in his movies, so Misery

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:56.880
director Rob Reiner decided to do the same,
though he is very hard to identify in his

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.780
appearance as the helicopter pilot.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:02.860
Hitchcock would not be the only legendary
director that Reiner took inspiration from,

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.410
there was also influence from Stanley Kubrick.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:09.360
At the end of Misery, we see Paul again 18
months later, and he’s finished a new novel

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.860
called The Higher Education of J Phillip Stone.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:16.339
Gotta wonder if this has anything to do Philip
Stone, an actor who often worked with Kubrick

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:20.360
and appeared in Barry Lyndon, A Clockwork
Orange and of perhaps most notably, The Shining.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.490
He’s the guy who tells Jack to “correct
them.”

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:27.440
“But I… ‘corrected’ them sir.”

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.930
If that one seems like a stretch, keep in
mind that at the time, there were only two

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.920
Stephen King novels that took place in his
fictional town of Sidewinder, Colorado, and

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.730
those two were Misery and The Shining.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:38.240
Let’s move on to the finale.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:44.200
“Remember how for all those years how nobody
knew who Misery’s real father was, or if

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.079
they’d ever be reunited?

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.940
It’s all right here.”

00:10:47.949 --> 00:10:51.740
This is an actual plot point in Misery’s
Return, the book within the Stephen King book,

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.480
but it happens much earlier, at the start
of what I assume is basically the second act

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.940
-- not at the end, like movie Paul writes
it out.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.320
Eventually, Paul finished it when he knocks
her out with the pig ornament, and this is

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:03.750
symbolic because the pig represents Misery.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.690
Annie has an actual pig of her own named Misery,
which Paul doesn’t like because he thought

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.800
he was done writing Misery novels, so he ends
up using Misery against her to finally do

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:12.940
away with his captor.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:16.440
There’s an interesting scene at the end,
where Annie reappears 18 months later while

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:19.900
Paul is at lunch with his editor, but it turns
out to just be in his imagination because

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.300
of the PTSD he acquired while staying with
her.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.200
This is meant to be kind of similar to the
book, where we’re given the impression that

00:11:25.209 --> 00:11:29.069
Annie survived and ambushed him at this apartment,
but that turns out to just be a writer’s

00:11:29.069 --> 00:11:33.329
scenario; it’s not really clear if he imagined
it because of the PTSD or if he was just letting

00:11:33.329 --> 00:11:36.600
his mind wander and write out a worst case
scenario type of scene.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:40.279
That part in the movie does a good job of
recreating that dynamic, because when Paul

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:43.880
sees her, he doesn’t panic, and it just
takes him a moment to concentrate and convince

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:45.660
himself that it’s not really Annie.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.120
Because he knows that she's dead.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:50.459
Also, the name of the woman who played the
waitress is Wendy Bowers.

00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:51.459
How great is that?

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:53.629
It’s like Wendy Torrance meets Henry Bowers.

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:55.830
And she claims to be his #1 fan.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.889
As the credits roll, we hear the Liberace
song, “I’ll Be Seeing You.”

00:11:58.889 --> 00:12:01.990
You may remember that Liberace was Annie’s
favorite musician.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:05.709
Annie Wilkes is the embodiment of the toxic
fan, which has become an even bigger topic

00:12:05.709 --> 00:12:09.450
of discussion in the years since Misery’s
release, so join me in the next episode of

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.100
Horror History where I’ll be analysing the
entire history of Annie Wilkes.

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.920
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:22.620
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one, assuming we stay inside.

